With \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) treated as AC grounds, \(M_2\) contributes only its output resistance \(r_{o2}\) from node \(X\) to ground. The output-side device is \(M_1\).

Let \(g_{oi}=1/r_{oi}\). Applying a test voltage \(v_o\) at the output gives

\[
\frac{i_o}{v_o}
=
\frac{g_{o1}g_{o2}}
{g_{m1}+g_{o1}+g_{o2}}.
\]

Therefore,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=
\frac{g_{m1}+g_{o1}+g_{o2}}{g_{o1}g_{o2}}
=
r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
}
\]

For \(g_{m1}r_{oi}\gg 1\),

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}.}
\]

If body effect of \(M_1\) is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\).

Assuming \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) are AC grounds and both MOSFETs are in saturation, let \(r_{oi}=1/g_{oi}\).

\(M_2\) has both its gate and source at AC ground, so it contributes only \(r_{o2}\) at the intermediate node. \(M_1\) is the common-gate/cascode device.

Applying a test voltage at the output gives

\[
R_{\text{out}}
=\frac{g_{m1}+g_{o1}+g_{o2}}{g_{o1}g_{o2}}
\]

or equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
= r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
}
\]

For \(g_{m1}r_{oi}\gg 1\),

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}}
\]

and if the devices have equal \(r_o\),

\[
R_{\text{out}}\approx g_m r_o^2.
\]

If body effect of \(M_1\) matters, replace \(g_{m1}\) by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\).
